+# Experimental OS range prefetch
+
+`pg_yaap.scan_os_prefetch_pages` requests advisory readahead for a range owned by
+one YAAP parallel Scan Source. It is experimental and defaults to **0 (off)**;
+valid values are 0–16384 pages. With 8KiB pages, 1024/4096/12800 pages correspond
+to 8/32/100MiB. The existing request batch and pending-request quota remain
+separate: this hint does not create a 100MiB PG Buffer read operation.
+
+```sql
+SET pg_yaap.scan_read_stream=yaap;
+SET pg_yaap.scan_prefetch_requests=1;
+SET pg_yaap.scan_os_prefetch_pages=4096;
+-- Optional observations:
+SET pg_yaap.profile_scan_io=detail;
+SET pg_yaap.profile_scan_blocks=65536;
+```
+
+The feature applies to buffered, shared, parallel YAAP heap scans. It does not
+change PG's native ReadStream mode. Unsupported paths such as direct data I/O,
+local/temp relations or a nonparallel scan do not issue hints. A disabled or
+unsupported path must not be interpreted as successful prefetch.
+
+Each worker still claims disjoint ranges using the existing PG parallel Block
+allocator. The initial hint covers up to the configured distance within its
+owned chunk. As the Block generator advances, later nonoverlapping hints extend
+the lead, without extending past the owned chunk. Relation-end wrap is split
+into physical ranges; PG's storage manager handles relation segment boundaries.
+The generator is ahead of actual tuple consumption, so this is generator-based
+lookahead rather than a timestamp of when the page is needed.
+
+The path uses PostgreSQL `smgrprefetch`/`FilePrefetch`. On this macOS build that
+ultimately invokes `F_RDADVISE`; other builds may use their own supported PG
+prefetch implementation. These are **advisory requests to the OS**, not confirmed
+completion or cache residency. The `smgrprefetch` return value also does not
+provide a per-page completion result; a zero failed-call count is not a guarantee
+that the OS performed all requested readahead.
+
+No extra Buffer pins are taken. The data continues through PG Buffer Manager,
+page validation and normal heap visibility before YAAP consumes it. No private
+raw-file buffer, independent physical I/O workers, or MVCC bypass is introduced.
+A SQL LIMIT can leave hinted pages unused, which is normal speculative work.
+
+## Bounded observations
+
+The existing Scan I/O profile reports per-source and aggregate
+`os_prefetch_calls`, `os_prefetch_pages_hinted`, `os_prefetch_ns`,
+`os_prefetch_failed_calls` and `os_prefetch_chunks`, together with requested page
+distance and effective enablement metadata. Elapsed time covers the hint API,
+not the storage completion latency. The counter is for calls to PG's advisory
+API, not physical device requests or bytes read from SSD.
+
+The optional bounded Block log adds `OS_PREFETCH` events with a physical Block
+range and API entry/return timestamps. It is written to the PG server log at
+normal Source teardown. Events remain subject to that trace's per-source cap
+and dropped count. Use it with `ASSIGN` events to verify ownership; request ID0
+means an advisory range, not a normal `StartReadBuffers` operation.
+
+## Resource tradeoff and benchmark criteria
+
+A large per-source distance multiplies across sources: twelve sources requesting
+100MiB ahead nominally cover about 1.2GiB. This is not reserved private memory,
+but it can increase OS cache pressure, speculative reads, and interference with
+other queries. The OS can coalesce, delay or ignore hints. Bigger is not assumed
+better. Keep the default off unless a representative, repeated end-to-end query
+comparison shows a benefit. A shorter subsequent `preadv` duration alone may
+only move waiting or I/O work earlier.
+
+Measure query wall time, device throughput and the existing PG read/wait counters
+with and without hints. Separate detailed profiling from performance baselines,
+keep request size/quota and worker counts fixed, and state cache conditions.
+
+## Integration test
+
+Use a dedicated instance with a rebuilt library and readable, nonrotating server
+log, without concurrent YAAP queries:
+
+```sh
+PGHOST=/tmp PGPORT=55432 PGUSER=postgres PGDATABASE=tpch \
+YAAP_TEST_OUTPUT=artifacts/scan-os-prefetch-regression \
+YAAP_BLOCK_TRACE_LOG=/absolute/path/to/postgresql-server.log \
+python3 tests/scan_os_prefetch.py
+```
+
+The test reuses existing fixture/DDL and trace/profile validators. It checks
+native-result equivalence, off/native mode, one and four workers, 32/256/1024-page
+settings, a small queue/pin window, rejected rows, empty input and LIMIT. It
+checks every hint is inside an assigned range, split at physical relation end,
+bounded by distance and not duplicated. Complete scans hint/consume each table
+page once; LIMIT is allowed to hint more than it consumes. Test-only PG combine
+limit16 makes the small fixture exercise multiple ownership chunks; production
+benchmark request size is unchanged.
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/scan_os_prefetch.py --self-test
+```
+
+The standalone range checks include wrapped ownership and reject out-of-owner,
+cross-EOF, overlapping and oversized hints. The live small fixture is generally
+hot; it verifies bounds/correctness, not cold-cache performance or OS completion.
